## an4.45
an4.45:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery.
an4.45:1.2 | S: Then, late at night, the glorious godling Rohitassa, lighting up the entire Jeta’s Grove, went up to the Buddha, bowed, stood to one side, and said to him: | T: Then Rohitassa, the son of a deva, in the far extreme of the night, his extreme radiance lighting up the entirety of Jeta’s Grove, went to the Blessed One. On arrival, having bowed down to the Blessed One, he stood to one side. As he was standing there he said to the Blessed One:
an4.45:2.1 | S: “Sir, is it possible to know or see or reach the end of the world by traveling to a place where there’s no being born, growing old, dying, passing away, or being reborn?” | T: “Is it possible, lord, by traveling, to know or see or reach a far end of the cosmos where one doesn’t take birth, age, die, pass away or reappear?”
an4.45:2.2 | S: “Reverend, I say it’s not possible to know or see or reach the end of the world by traveling to a place where there’s no being born, growing old, dying, passing away, or being reborn.” | T: “I tell you, friend, that it isn’t possible by traveling to know or see or reach a far end of the cosmos where one doesn’t take birth, age, die, pass away, or reappear.”
an4.45:3.1 | S: “It’s incredible, sir, it’s amazing, | T: “It’s amazing, lord, and astounding,
an4.45:3.2 | S: how well said this was by the Buddha. | T: how well that has been said by the Blessed One:
an4.45:3.3 | P: ‘yattha kho, āvuso, na jāyati na jīyati na mīyati na cavati na upapajjati, nāhaṁ taṁ gamanena lokassa antaṁ ñāteyyaṁ daṭṭheyyaṁ patteyyanti vadāmī’”ti. | T: ‘I tell you, friend, that it isn’t possible by traveling to know or see or reach a far end of the cosmos where one doesn’t take birth, age, die, pass away, or reappear.’
an4.45:4.1 | S: Once upon a time, I was a seer named Rohitassa, son of the benefactors. I was a sky-walker with psychic powers. | T: Once I was a seer named Rohitassa, a student of Bhoja, a powerful sky-walker.
an4.45:4.2 | S: I was as fast as a light arrow easily shot across the shadow of a palm tree by a well-trained expert archer with a strong bow. | T: My speed was as fast as that of a strong archer—well trained, a practiced hand, a practiced sharpshooter—shooting a light arrow across the shadow of a palm tree.
an4.45:4.3 | S: My stride was such that it could span from the eastern ocean to the western ocean. | T: My stride stretched as far as the east sea is from the west.
an4.45:4.4 | S: Having such speed and stride, this wish came to me: | T: To me, endowed with such speed, such a stride, there came the desire:
an4.45:4.5 | S: ‘I will reach the end of the world by traveling.’ | T: ‘I will go traveling to the end of the cosmos.’
an4.45:4.6 | S: I traveled for my whole lifespan of a hundred years—pausing only to eat and drink, go to the toilet, and sleep to dispel weariness—and I passed away along the way, never reaching the end of the world. | T: I—with a one-hundred year life, a one-hundred year span—spent one hundred years traveling—apart from the time spent on eating, drinking, chewing & tasting, urinating & defecating, and sleeping to fight off weariness—but without reaching the end of the cosmos I died along the way.
an4.45:5.1 | S: It’s incredible, sir, it’s amazing, | T: So it’s amazing, lord, and astounding,
an4.45:5.2 | S: how well said this was by the Buddha.” | T: how well that has been said by the Blessed One:
an4.45:5.3 | P: ‘yattha kho, āvuso, na jāyati na jīyati na mīyati na cavati na upapajjati, nāhaṁ taṁ gamanena lokassa antaṁ ñāteyyaṁ daṭṭheyyaṁ patteyyanti vadāmī’”ti. | T: ‘I tell you, friend, that it isn’t possible by traveling to know or see or reach a far end of the cosmos where one doesn’t take birth, age, die, pass away, or reappear.’”
an4.45:6.1 | S: “Reverend, I say it’s not possible to know or see or reach the end of the world by traveling to a place where there’s no being born, growing old, dying, passing away, or being reborn. | T: (When this was said, the Blessed One responded:) “I tell you, friend, that it isn’t possible by traveling to know or see or reach a far end of the cosmos where one doesn’t take birth, age, die, pass away, or reappear.
an4.45:6.2 | S: But I also say there’s no making an end of suffering without reaching the end of the world. | T: But at the same time, I tell you that there is no making an end of suffering & stress without reaching the end of the cosmos.
an4.45:6.3 | S: For it is in this fathom-long carcass with its perception and mind that I describe the world, its origin, its cessation, and the practice that leads to its cessation. | T: Yet it is just within this fathom-long body, with its perception & intellect, that I declare that there is the cosmos, the origination of the cosmos, the cessation of the cosmos, and the path of practice leading to the cessation of the cosmos.”
an4.45:7.1 | S: The end of the world can never | T: It’s not to be reached by traveling, the end of the cosmos— regardless.
an4.45:7.2 | S: be reached by traveling. | T: (none)
an4.45:7.3 | S: But without reaching the end of the world, | T: And it’s not without reaching the end of the cosmos
an4.45:7.4 | S: there’s no release from suffering. | T: that there is release from suffering & stress.
an4.45:8.1 | S: So an intelligent person, understanding the world, | T: So, truly, an expert with regard to the cosmos, intelligent,
an4.45:8.2 | S: has completed the spiritual journey and gone to the end of the world. | T: a knower of the end of the cosmos, having fulfilled the holy life,
an4.45:8.3 | S: Assuaged, knowing the end of the world, | T: knowing the cosmos’ end, concentrated,
an4.45:8.4 | S: they do not hope for this world or the next.” | T: doesn’t long for this cosmos or any other.
an4.45:8.5 | P: Pañcamaṁ. | T: (none)

## thag12.2
thag12.2:1.1 | S: I was born in a low-class family. | T: Into a lowly family I was born,
thag12.2:1.2 | S: We were poor, with little to eat. | T: poor, with next to no food.
thag12.2:1.3 | S: My job was lowly— | T: My work was degrading:
thag12.2:1.4 | S: I threw out the old flowers. | T: I gathered the spoiled, the withered flowers from shrines and threw them away.
thag12.2:2.1 | S: Shunned by people, | T: People found me disgusting,
thag12.2:2.2 | S: I was disregarded and held in contempt. | T: despised me, disparaged me.
thag12.2:2.3 | S: I humbled my heart | T: Lowering my heart,
thag12.2:2.4 | S: and paid respects to many people. | T: I showed reverence to many.
thag12.2:3.1 | S: Then I saw the Buddha | T: Then I saw the One Self-awakened,
thag12.2:3.2 | S: at the fore of the mendicant Saṅgha. | T: arrayed with a squadron of monks,
thag12.2:3.3 | S: The great hero | T: the Great Hero,
thag12.2:3.4 | S: was entering the capital city of the Magadhans. | T: entering the city, supreme, of the Magadhans.
thag12.2:4.1 | S: I dropped my flail | T: Throwing down my carrying pole,
thag12.2:4.2 | S: and approached to pay homage. | T: I approached him to do reverence.
thag12.2:4.3 | S: Out of sympathy for me, | T: He—
thag12.2:4.4 | S: the supreme man stood still. | T: the supreme man—stood still out of sympathy just for me.
thag12.2:5.1 | S: When I had paid homage at the Teacher’s feet, | T: After paying homage to the feet of the teacher,
thag12.2:5.2 | S: I stood to one side | T: I stood to one side
thag12.2:5.3 | S: and requested the going forth | T: & requested the Going Forth from him,
thag12.2:5.4 | S: from the one supreme among all beings. | T: supreme among all living beings.
thag12.2:6.1 | S: Consequently, the Teacher, being compassionate, | T: The compassionate Teacher,
thag12.2:6.2 | S: having sympathy for the whole world, | T: sympathetic to all the world,
thag12.2:6.3 | S: said to me, “Come, monk!” | T: said: “Come, monk.”
thag12.2:6.4 | S: That was my ordination. | T: That was my formal Acceptance.
thag12.2:7.1 | S: Staying alone in the wilderness, | T: Alone, I stayed in the wilds,
thag12.2:7.2 | S: meditating tirelessly, | T: untiring,
thag12.2:7.3 | S: I have completed what the Teacher taught, | T: I followed the Teacher’s words, just as he, the Conqueror, had taught me.
thag12.2:7.4 | S: just as the victor advised me. | T: (none)
thag12.2:8.1 | S: In the first watch of the night, | T: In the first watch of the night,
thag12.2:8.2 | S: I recollected my past lives. | T: I recollected previous lives;
thag12.2:8.3 | S: In the middle watch, | T: in the middle watch,
thag12.2:8.4 | S: I purified my clairvoyance. | T: purified the divine eye;
thag12.2:8.5 | S: In the last watch, | T: in the last,
thag12.2:8.6 | S: I shattered the mass of darkness. | T: burst the mass of darkness.
thag12.2:9.1 | S: At the end of the night, | T: Then, as night was ending
thag12.2:9.2 | S: as the sunrise drew near, | T: & the sun returning,
thag12.2:9.3 | S: Indra and the Divinity came | T: Indra & Brahmā came to pay homage to me,
thag12.2:9.4 | S: and revered me with joined hands. | T: hands palm-to-palm at their hearts:
thag12.2:10.1 | S: “Homage to you, O thoroughbred! | T: “Homage to you, O thoroughbred of men,
thag12.2:10.2 | S: Homage to you, supreme among men! | T: Homage to you, O man supreme,
thag12.2:10.3 | S: Since your defilements are ended, | T: whose effluents are ended.
thag12.2:10.4 | S: you, good fellow, are worthy of a religious donation.” | T: You, dear sir, are worthy of offerings.”
thag12.2:11.1 | S: Consequently, having seen me honored | T: Seeing me,
thag12.2:11.2 | S: by the assembly of gods, | T: arrayed with a squadron of devas,
thag12.2:11.3 | S: the Teacher smiled | T: the Teacher smiled & said:
thag12.2:11.4 | S: and said the following: | T: (none)
thag12.2:12.1 | S: “By fervor and spiritual practice, | T: “Through austerity, celibacy,
thag12.2:12.2 | S: by restraint and by self-control: | T: restraint, & self-control:
thag12.2:12.3 | S: that’s how to become a brahmin, | T: That’s how one is a brahman.
thag12.2:12.4 | S: this is the supreme brahmin.” | T: He is a brahman supreme.”
thag12.2:12.5 | P: … Sunīto thero … | T: (none)
thag12.2:12.6 | P: Dvādasakanipāto niṭṭhito. | T: (none)
thag12.2:13.0 | P: Tatruddānaṁ | T: (none)
thag12.2:13.1 | P: Sīlavā ca sunīto ca, | T: (none)
thag12.2:13.2 | P: therā dve te mahiddhikā; | T: (none)
thag12.2:13.3 | P: Dvādasamhi nipātamhi, | T: (none)
thag12.2:13.4 | P: gāthāyo catuvīsatīti. | T: (none)

