## sn35.246
sn35.246:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, any monk or nun who has desire or greed or hate or delusion or aversion come up for sights known by the eye should shield their mind from them: | T: “Monks, in whatever monk or nun there arises desire, passion, aversion, delusion, or mental resistance with regard to forms cognizable via the eye, he/she should hold the mind in check.
sn35.246:1.2 | S: ‘This path is dangerous and perilous, thorny and tangled; it’s a wrong turn, a bad path, in poor condition. | T: (Thinking,) ‘It’s dangerous & dubious, that path, thorny & overgrown, a miserable path, a devious path, impenetrable.
sn35.246:1.3 | S: This path is frequented by untrue persons, not by true persons. | T: It’s a path followed by people of no integrity, not a path followed by people of integrity.
sn35.246:1.4 | S: It’s not worthy of you.’ | T: It’s not worthy of you,’
sn35.246:1.5 | S: The mind should be shielded from this when it comes to sights known by the eye. | T: he/she should hold the mind in check with regard to forms cognizable via the eye.
sn35.246:1.6 | S: Any monk or nun who has desire or greed or hate or delusion or repulsion come up for sounds … smells … tastes … touches … | T: “In whatever monk or nun there arises desire, passion, aversion, delusion, or mental resistance with regard to sounds cognizable via the ear… aromas cognizable via the nose… flavors cognizable via the tongue… tactile sensations cognizable via the body…
sn35.246:1.7 | S: ideas known by the mind should shield their mind against them: | T: ideas cognizable via the intellect, he/she should hold the mind in check.
sn35.246:1.8 | S: ‘This path is dangerous and perilous, thorny and tangled; it’s a wrong turn, a bad path, a sorry state. | T: (Thinking,) ‘It’s dangerous & dubious, that path, thorny & overgrown, a miserable path, a devious path, impenetrable.
sn35.246:1.9 | S: This path is frequented by untrue persons, not by true persons. | T: It’s a path followed by people of no integrity, not a path followed by people of integrity.
sn35.246:1.10 | S: It’s not worthy of you.’ | T: It’s not worthy of you,’
sn35.246:1.11 | S: The mind should be shielded from this when it comes to ideas known by the mind. | T: he/she should hold the mind in check with regard to ideas cognizable via the intellect.
sn35.246:2.1 | S: Suppose the crops have ripened, | T: “Suppose that corn had ripened
sn35.246:2.2 | S: but the caretaker is negligent. If an ox fond of crops invades the crops they’d indulge themselves as much as they like. | T: and the watchman was heedless. A corn-eating ox, invading the corn to eat it, would intoxicate itself as much as it liked.
sn35.246:2.3 | S: In the same way, when an unlearned ordinary person doesn’t exercise restraint when it comes to the six fields of contact, they indulge themselves in the five kinds of sensual stimulation as much as they like. | T: In the same way, an uninstructed run-of-the-mill person, not exercising restraint with regard to the six media of sensory contact, intoxicates himself with the five strings of sensuality as much as he likes.
sn35.246:3.1 | S: Suppose the crops have ripened, and the caretaker is diligent. If an ox fond of crops invades the crops | T: “Now suppose that corn had ripened and the watchman was heedful. The corn-eating ox would invade the corn to eat it,
sn35.246:3.2 | S: the caretaker would grab them firmly by the muzzle. | T: but then the watchman would grab it firmly by the muzzle.
sn35.246:3.3 | S: Then they’d grab them above the hump and hold them fast there. | T: Having grabbed it firmly by the muzzle, he would pin it down by the forehead.
sn35.246:3.4 | S: Then they’d give them a good thrashing | T: Having pinned it down by the forehead, he would give it a sound thrashing with a stick.
sn35.246:3.5 | S: before driving them away. | T: Having given it a sound thrashing with a stick, he would let it go.
sn35.246:3.6 | S: For a second time, | T: “A second time…
sn35.246:3.7 | S: and even a third time, the same thing might happen. | T: A third time, the corn-eating ox would invade the corn to eat it,
sn35.246:3.8 | P: Tamenaṁ kiṭṭhārakkho nāsāyaṁ suggahitaṁ gaṇheyya. | T: but then the watchman would grab it firmly by the muzzle.
sn35.246:3.9 | P: Nāsāyaṁ suggahitaṁ gahetvā uparighaṭāyaṁ suniggahitaṁ niggaṇheyya. | T: Having grabbed it firmly by the muzzle, he would pin it down by the forehead.
sn35.246:3.10 | P: Uparighaṭāyaṁ suniggahitaṁ niggahetvā daṇḍena sutāḷitaṁ tāḷeyya. | T: Having pinned it down by the forehead, he would give it a sound thrashing with a stick.
sn35.246:3.11 | P: Daṇḍena sutāḷitaṁ tāḷetvā osajjeyya. | T: Having given it a sound thrashing with a stick, he would let it go.
sn35.246:3.12 | S: As a result, no matter how often they might stand or sit in a village or wilderness, that ox fond of crops would never invade that crop again, | T: “As a result, the corn-eating ox—regardless of whether it went to the village or to the wilds, was standing still or lying down—wouldn’t invade the corn again,
sn35.246:3.13 | S: remembering the beating they got earlier. | T: because it would recall the earlier taste it got of the stick.
sn35.246:3.14 | S: In the same way, when a mendicant’s mind is straightened up, well straightened up when it comes to the six fields of contact, becomes stilled internally; it settles, unifies, and becomes immersed in samādhi. | T: “In the same way, when a monk’s mind is held back, thoroughly held back, from the six media of sensory contact, his mind settles inwardly, grows steady, unified, & concentrated.
sn35.246:4.1 | S: Suppose a king or their chief minister had never heard the sound of an arched harp. | T: “Suppose there were a king or king’s minister who had never heard the sound of a lute before.
sn35.246:4.2 | S: When he first hears the sound, | T: He might hear the sound of a lute and say,
sn35.246:4.3 | S: he’d say, | T: (none)
sn35.246:4.4 | S: ‘Oh my, what is making this sound, so arousing, sensuous, intoxicating, infatuating, and captivating?’ | T: ‘What, my good men, is that sound—so delightful, so tantalizing, so intoxicating, so ravishing, so enthralling?’
sn35.246:4.5 | S: They’d say to him, | T: They would say,
sn35.246:4.6 | S: ‘That, sir, is an arched harp.’ | T: ‘That, sire, is called a lute, whose sound is so delightful, so tantalizing, so intoxicating, so ravishing, so enthralling.’
sn35.246:4.7 | S: He’d say, | T: Then he would say,
sn35.246:4.8 | S: ‘Go, worthy sir, fetch me that arched harp.’ | T: ‘Go & fetch me that lute.’
sn35.246:4.9 | S: So they’d fetch it | T: They would fetch the lute and say,
sn35.246:4.10 | S: and say, | T: (none)
sn35.246:4.11 | S: ‘This, sir, is that arched harp.’ | T: ‘Here, sire, is the lute whose sound is so delightful, so tantalizing, so intoxicating, so ravishing, so enthralling.’
sn35.246:4.12 | S: He’d say, | T: He would say,
sn35.246:4.13 | S: ‘I’ve had enough of that arched harp! Just fetch me the sound.’ | T: ‘Enough of your lute. Fetch me just the sound.’
sn35.246:4.14 | S: They’d say, | T: Then they would say,
sn35.246:4.15 | S: ‘Sir, this arched harp is made of many components assembled together, | T: ‘This lute, sire, is made of numerous components, a great many components.
sn35.246:4.16 | S: which make a sound when they’re played. That is, | T: It’s through the activity of numerous components that it sounds: that is,
sn35.246:4.17 | S: it depends on the soundbox, the leather resonator, the neck, the tuning cords, the strings, the plectrum, and a person to play it properly. That’s how an arched harp is made of many components assembled together, | T: in dependence on the body, the skin, the neck, the frame, the strings, the bridge, and the appropriate human effort. Thus it is that this lute—made of numerous components, a great many components—
sn35.246:4.18 | S: which make a sound when they’re played.’ | T: sounds through the activity of numerous components.’
sn35.246:4.19 | S: But he’d split that harp into ten pieces or a hundred pieces, then splinter it up. | T: “Then the king would split the lute into ten pieces, a hundred pieces. Having split the lute into ten pieces, a hundred pieces, he would shave it to splinters.
sn35.246:4.20 | S: He’d burn the splinters with fire, and reduce them to ashes. | T: Having shaved it to splinters, he would burn it in a fire. Having burned it in a fire, he would reduce it to ashes.
sn35.246:4.21 | S: Then he’d whisk away the ashes in a strong wind, or glide them down a swift stream. | T: Having reduced it to ashes, he would winnow it before a high wind or let it be washed away by a swift-flowing stream.
sn35.246:4.22 | S: Then he’d say, | T: He would then say,
sn35.246:4.23 | S: ‘It seems that there’s nothing to this thing called an arched harp or whatever’s called an arched harp! But people waste their time with it, negligent and heedless!’ | T: ‘A sorry thing, this lute—whatever a lute may be—by which people have been so thoroughly tricked & deceived.’
sn35.246:4.24 | S: In the same way, a mendicant searches for form, feeling, perception, choices, and consciousness anywhere they might be reborn. | T: “In the same way, a monk investigates form, however far form may go. He investigates feeling… perception… fabrications… consciousness, however far consciousness may go.
sn35.246:4.25 | S: As they search in this way, | T: As he is investigating form… feeling…
sn35.246:4.26 | P: saññaṁ … | T: perception…
sn35.246:4.27 | P: saṅkhāre … | T: fabrications…
sn35.246:4.28 | P: viññāṇaṁ samanvesato yāvatā viññāṇassa gati. | T: consciousness, however far consciousness may go,
sn35.246:4.29 | S: their ideas of ‘I’ or ‘mine’ or ‘I am’ are no more.” | T: any thoughts of ‘me’ or ‘mine’ or ‘I am’ do not occur to him.”
sn35.246:4.30 | P: Navamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an4.52
an4.52:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, there are these four kinds of overflowing merit, overflowing goodness. They nurture happiness and are conducive to heaven, ripening in happiness and leading to heaven. They lead to what is likable, desirable, agreeable, to welfare and happiness. | T: “Monks, these four bonanzas of merit, bonanzas of skillfulness, nourishments of bliss—heavenly, resulting in bliss—lead to what is wished for, appealing, agreeable, well-being, & bliss.
an4.52:1.2 | S: What four? | T: Which four?
an4.52:1.3 | S: It’s when a noble disciple has experiential confidence in the Buddha: | T: “There is the case where the disciple of the noble ones is endowed with verified confidence in the Awakened One:
an4.52:1.4 | S: ‘That Blessed One is perfected, a fully awakened Buddha, accomplished in knowledge and conduct, holy, knower of the world, supreme guide for those fit for training, teacher of gods and humans, awakened, blessed.’ | T: ‘Indeed, the Blessed One is worthy & rightly self-awakened, consummate in clear-knowing & conduct, well-gone, an expert with regard to the cosmos, unexcelled trainer of people fit to be tamed, teacher of devas & human beings, awakened, blessed.’
an4.52:1.5 | S: This is the first kind of overflowing merit … | T: This is the first bonanza of merit, bonanza of skillfulness, nourishment of bliss—heavenly, resulting in bliss—that leads to what is wished for, appealing, agreeable, well-being, & bliss.
an4.52:2.1 | S: Furthermore, a noble disciple has experiential confidence in the teaching: | T: “And further, the disciple of the noble ones is endowed with verified confidence in the Dhamma:
an4.52:2.2 | S: ‘The teaching is well explained by the Buddha—apparent in the present life, immediately effective, inviting inspection, relevant, so that sensible people can know it for themselves.’ | T: ‘The Dhamma is well taught by the Blessed One, to be seen here & now, timeless, inviting verification, pertinent, to be experienced by the observant for themselves.’
an4.52:2.3 | S: This is the second kind of overflowing merit … | T: This is the second bonanza of merit, bonanza of skillfulness, nourishment of bliss—heavenly, resulting in bliss—that leads to what is wished for, appealing, agreeable, well-being, & bliss.
an4.52:3.1 | S: Furthermore, a noble disciple has experiential confidence in the Saṅgha: | T: “And further, the disciple of the noble ones is endowed with verified confidence in the Saṅgha:
an4.52:3.2 | S: ‘The Saṅgha of the Buddha’s disciples is practicing the way that’s good, direct, systematic, and proper. It consists of the four pairs, the eight individual persons. This is the Saṅgha of the Buddha’s disciples that is worthy of offerings dedicated to the gods, worthy of hospitality, worthy of a religious donation, worthy of greeting with cupped palms, and is the supreme field of merit for the world.’ | T: ‘The Saṅgha of the Blessed One’s disciples who have practiced well… who have practiced straight-forwardly… who have practiced methodically… who have practiced masterfully—in other words, the four types of noble disciples when taken as pairs, the eight when taken as individual types—they are the Saṅgha of the Blessed One’s disciples: worthy of gifts, worthy of hospitality, worthy of offerings, worthy of respect, the incomparable field of merit for the world.’
an4.52:3.3 | S: This is the third kind of overflowing merit … | T: This is the third bonanza of merit, bonanza of skillfulness, nourishment of bliss—heavenly, resulting in bliss—that leads to what is wished for, appealing, agreeable, well-being, & bliss.
an4.52:4.1 | S: Furthermore, a noble disciple’s ethical conduct is loved by the noble ones, unbroken, impeccable, spotless, and unmarred, liberating, praised by sensible people, not mistaken, and leading to immersion. | T: “And further, the disciple of the noble ones is endowed with virtues that are appealing to the noble ones: untorn, unbroken, unspotted, unsplattered, liberating, praised by the observant, ungrasped at, leading to concentration.
an4.52:4.2 | S: This is the fourth kind of overflowing merit … | T: This is the fourth bonanza of merit, bonanza of skillfulness, nourishment of bliss—heavenly, resulting in bliss—that leads to what is wished for, appealing, agreeable, well-being, & bliss.
an4.52:4.3 | S: These are the four kinds of overflowing merit, overflowing goodness. They nurture happiness and are conducive to heaven, ripening in happiness and leading to heaven. They lead to what is likable, desirable, agreeable, to welfare and happiness. | T: “These are four bonanzas of merit, bonanzas of skillfulness, nourishments of bliss—heavenly, resulting in bliss—that lead to what is wished for, appealing, agreeable, well-being, & bliss.”
an4.52:5.1 | S: Whoever has faith in the Realized One, | T: ‘One whose conviction in the Tathāgata
an4.52:5.2 | S: unwavering and well grounded; | T: is well-established, unshakable;
an4.52:5.3 | S: whose ethical conduct is good, | T: whose virtue is admirable,
an4.52:5.4 | S: praised and loved by the noble ones; | T: appealing to the noble ones, praised;
an4.52:6.1 | S: who has confidence in the Saṅgha, | T: who has confidence in the Saṅgha,
an4.52:6.2 | S: and correct view: | T: & vision made straight:
an4.52:6.3 | S: they’re said to be prosperous, | T: “Not poor,” they say of him.
an4.52:6.4 | S: their life is not in vain. | T: Not in vain his life.
an4.52:7.1 | S: So let the wise devote themselves | T: (none)
an4.52:7.2 | S: to faith, ethical behavior, | T: So conviction & virtue,
an4.52:7.3 | S: confidence, and insight into the teaching, | T: confidence & Dhamma-vision should be cultivated by the intelligent,
an4.52:7.4 | S: remembering the instructions of the Buddhas.” | T: remembering the Buddhas’ teachings.’”
an4.52:7.5 | P: Dutiyaṁ. | T: (none)

## snp1.6
snp1.6:1.1 | S: So I have heard. | T: I have heard that at one time the Blessed One was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery.
snp1.6:1.2 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Sāvatthī in Jeta’s Grove, Anāthapiṇḍika’s monastery. | T: (none)
snp1.6:1.3 | S: Then, late at night, a glorious deity, lighting up the entire Jeta’s Grove, went up to the Buddha, bowed, and stood to one side. | T: Then a certain deva, in the far extreme of the night, her extreme radiance lighting up the entirety of Jeta’s Grove, went to the Blessed One. On arrival, having bowed down to him, she stood to one side.
snp1.6:1.4 | S: Standing to one side, that deity addressed the Buddha in verse: | T: As she was standing there, she addressed him with a verse:
snp1.6:2.1 | S: “We ask Gotama | T: About the man in decline we ask Gotama,
snp1.6:2.2 | S: about a man’s downfall. | T: (none)
snp1.6:2.3 | S: We have come to ask you, sir: | T: having come to question the Blessed One:
snp1.6:2.4 | S: what leads to downfall?” | T: What is the way leading to decline?
snp1.6:3.1 | S: “It’s easy to understand success, | T: The Buddha: Easily known is the one of good prospects;
snp1.6:3.2 | S: understanding downfall is just as easy. | T: easily known, the one in decline.
snp1.6:3.3 | S: One who loves the teaching succeeds, | T: The one of good prospects loves the Dhamma,
snp1.6:3.4 | S: but a hater of the teaching meets their downfall.” | T: the one in decline detests it.
snp1.6:4.1 | S: “We understand what you’re saying, | T: The deva: We know, indeed, that that is so.

