## sn5.4
sn5.4:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: Near Sāvatthī.
sn5.4:1.2 | S: Then the nun Vijayā robed up in the morning … | T: Then, early in the morning, Vijayā the nun adjusted her robes and, taking her bowl & outer robe, went into Sāvatthī for alms. When she had gone for alms in Sāvatthī and had returned from her alms round, after her meal she went to the Grove of the Blind to spend the day. Having gone deep into the Grove of the Blind,
sn5.4:1.3 | S: and sat at the root of a tree for the day’s meditation. | T: she sat down at the foot of a tree for the day’s abiding.
sn5.4:1.4 | S: Then Māra the Wicked, wanting to make the nun Vijayā feel fear, terror, and goosebumps, wanting to make her fall away from immersion, went up to her and addressed her in verse: | T: Then Māra the Evil One, wanting to arouse fear, horripilation, & terror in her, wanting to make her fall away from concentration, approached her & addressed her in verse:
sn5.4:2.1 | S: “You’re so young and beautiful, | T: “You, a beautiful young woman.
sn5.4:2.2 | S: and I’m a youth in my prime. | T: I, a young man.
sn5.4:2.3 | S: Come, my lady, let us enjoy | T: Come, my lady, let’s enjoy ourselves
sn5.4:2.4 | S: the music of a five-piece band.” | T: to the music of a five-piece band.”
sn5.4:3.1 | S: Then the nun Vijayā thought, | T: Then the thought occurred to Vijayā the nun:
sn5.4:3.2 | S: “Who’s speaking this verse, a human or a non-human?” | T: “Now who has recited this verse—a human being or a non-human one?”
sn5.4:3.3 | S: Then she thought, | T: Then it occurred to her:
sn5.4:3.4 | S: “This is Māra the Wicked, wanting to make me feel fear, terror, and goosebumps, wanting to make me fall away from immersion!” | T: “This is Māra the Evil One who has recited this verse wanting to arouse fear, horripilation, & terror in me, wanting to make me fall away from concentration.”
sn5.4:3.5 | S: Then Vijayā, knowing that this was Māra the Wicked, replied to him in verse: | T: Then, having understood that “This is Māra the Evil One,” she replied to him in verses:
sn5.4:4.1 | S: “Sights, sounds, tastes, smells, | T: “Lovely sights, sounds, smells, tastes,
sn5.4:4.2 | S: and touches so delightful. | T: & tactile sensations
sn5.4:4.3 | S: I hand them right back to you, Māra, | T: I leave to you, Māra.
sn5.4:4.4 | S: for I have no use for them. | T: I have no need for them.
sn5.4:5.1 | S: This body is rotting, | T: I’m disgusted, ashamed of this putrid body—
sn5.4:5.2 | S: decaying and frail. | T: disintegrating, dissolving.
sn5.4:5.3 | S: I’m horrified and repelled by it, | T: (none)
sn5.4:5.4 | S: and I’ve eradicated sensual craving. | T: Sensual craving is rooted out.
sn5.4:6.1 | S: There are beings in the realm of luminous form, | T: Beings who have come to form,
sn5.4:6.2 | S: and others stuck in the formless. | T: & those with a share in the formless,
sn5.4:6.3 | S: I’ve destroyed the darkness | T: & the peaceful attainments:
sn5.4:6.4 | S: regarding all such peaceful attainments.” | T: their darkness is completely destroyed.”
sn5.4:7.1 | S: Then Māra the Wicked, thinking, “The nun Vijayā knows me!” miserable and sad, vanished right there. | T: Then Māra the Evil One—sad & dejected at realizing, “Vijayā the nun knows me”—vanished right there.

## sn5.5
sn5.5:1.1 | S: At Sāvatthī. | T: Near Sāvatthī.
sn5.5:1.2 | S: Then the nun Uppalavaṇṇā robed up in the morning … | T: Then, early in the morning, Uppalavaṇṇā the nun adjusted her robes and, taking her bowl & outer robe, went into Sāvatthī for alms. When she had gone for alms in Sāvatthī and had returned from her alms round, after her meal she went to the Grove of the Blind to spend the day.
sn5.5:1.3 | S: and stood at the root of a sal tree in full flower. | T: Having gone deep into the Grove of the Blind, she stood at the root of a flowering Sal tree.
sn5.5:1.4 | S: Then Māra the Wicked, wanting to make the nun Uppalavaṇṇā feel fear, terror, and goosebumps, wanting to make her fall away from immersion, went up to her and addressed her in verse: | T: Then Māra the Evil One, wanting to arouse fear, horripilation, & terror in her, wanting to make her fall away from concentration, approached her & addressed her in verse:
sn5.5:2.1 | S: “You’ve come to this sal tree all crowned with flowers, | T: “You’ve come, nun, to this Sal tree with its fine flowering crest,
sn5.5:2.2 | S: and stand at its root all alone, O nun. | T: and stand alone at its root,
sn5.5:2.3 | S: Your beauty is second to none— | T: with no one to match you in beauty.
sn5.5:2.4 | S: silly girl, aren’t you afraid of rascals?” | T: In your foolishness, aren’t you afraid of rape?”
sn5.5:3.1 | S: Then the nun Uppalavaṇṇā thought, | T: Then the thought occurred to Uppalavaṇṇā the nun:
sn5.5:3.2 | S: “Who’s speaking this verse, a human or a non-human?” | T: “Now who has recited this verse—a human being or a non-human one?”
sn5.5:3.3 | S: Then she thought, | T: Then it occurred to her:
sn5.5:3.4 | S: “This is Māra the Wicked, wanting to make me feel fear, terror, and goosebumps, wanting to make me fall away from immersion!” | T: “This is Māra the Evil One who has recited this verse wanting to arouse fear, horripilation, & terror in me, wanting to make me fall away from concentration.”
sn5.5:3.5 | S: Then Uppalavaṇṇā, knowing that this was Māra the Wicked, replied to him in verse: | T: Then, having understood that “This is Māra the Evil One,” she replied to him in verses:
sn5.5:4.1 | S: “Even if 100,000 rascals like you | T: “If even a hundred-thousand rapists came across me like this,
sn5.5:4.2 | S: were to come here, | T: (none)
sn5.5:4.3 | S: I’d stir not a hair nor panic. | T: I wouldn’t stir a hair. I’d feel no terror,
sn5.5:4.4 | S: I’m not scared of you, Māra, even alone. | T: and I’m not afraid of you, Māra, even alone like this.
sn5.5:5.1 | S: I’ll vanish, | T: Here—I disappear.
sn5.5:5.2 | S: or I’ll enter your belly; | T: I slip into your belly
sn5.5:5.3 | S: I could stand between your eyebrows | T: or stand between your eyebrows,
sn5.5:5.4 | S: and you still wouldn’t see me. | T: and you don’t see me.
sn5.5:6.1 | S: I’m the master of my own mind, | T: I have mastery over the mind,
sn5.5:6.2 | S: I’ve developed the bases of psychic power well. | T: have well-developed the bases of power.
sn5.5:6.3 | S: I’m free from all bonds, | T: I’m released from all bonds,
sn5.5:6.4 | S: and I’m not afraid of you, sir!” | T: and not afraid of you, my friend.”
sn5.5:7.1 | S: Then Māra the Wicked, thinking, “The nun Uppalavaṇṇā knows me!” miserable and sad, vanished right there. | T: Then Māra the Evil One—sad & dejected at realizing, “Uppalavaṇṇā the nun knows me”—vanished right there.


